energy-climb-1c01f8a6.interpretation:
The formula defines the redshift, denoted by the symbol 
'z'
. It is calculated as the difference between the observed wavelength (
\lambda_{obs}
) and the emitted wavelength (
\lambda_{emit}
), divided by the emitted wavelength (
\lambda_{emit}
).  A positive value of 
'z'
 indicates that the observed wavelength is longer than the emitted wavelength (redshift), implying that the source is moving away from the observer or that the light has traveled through a region of strong gravity. A negative value of 
'z'
 (blueshift) would indicate that the observed wavelength is shorter, meaning the source is moving towards the observer. The quantity 
'z'
 is dimensionless, as it is a ratio of wavelengths.

energy-climb-1c01f8a6.limits_and_boundary:
As 
\lambda_{obs}
 approaches 
\lambda_{emit}
, the redshift 
'z'
 approaches zero, indicating no relative motion or gravitational influence. If 
\lambda_{obs}
 becomes much larger than 
\lambda_{emit}
 (a large redshift), the value of 
'z'
 can become very large, signifying a significant recession velocity or a strong gravitational effect. The denominator 
\lambda_{emit}
 cannot be zero, as it represents a fundamental property of the emitted light.
